{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And he made it into a calf.</b> Meaning, because it was made through him — not that he himself made it, as it is written (Exodus 32:24): ״וַיֵּצֵא הָעֵגֶל הַזֶּה״. Or it may be that because he took the gold from their hand into his own hand, and was not wise enough to let them set the gold on the ground to nullify their sorceries (Zohar vol. II p. 192a), the verse attributes the deed to him." "<b>And they said: These are your Elohim, O Israel, who, etc.</b> It is difficult: how could crassness and brutishness persuade a discerning generation, numerous and distinguished, to say of an inanimate object ״these are your Elohim״, etc.? And even if the calf lowed through the medium of sorcery, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Tanhuma), even so this would only suffice to make people think something of it — but to testify a false testimony that this is the one who brought them out — no." "Yet the correct interpretation is that on the contrary, in these very words they revealed their intent: that they were not worshipping and making a god other than the one who brought them out of the land of Egypt, and they intended through this statement to establish in it an aspect of divinity corresponding to the one who brought them out, and the worship would be to a portion of the whole — and thus they would be as though worshipping the whole. And the portion would go before them and it would answer when something was requested of it. And it was not foreign in their eyes, for does not man possess a divine portion from above? But rather, since its composition was not of a species that endures, the spirit would return to Elohim. So they contrived to do this thing in order to draw supernal power into something that endures, and it would be constantly available before them. And they never uprooted ״אָנֹכִי ה׳… אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתִיךָ מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״ but said <b>these</b>. And perhaps it is for this reason that it says <b>who brought you up</b> in the plural, to allude to a lower force as well as the upper one — perish the thought — and through this they blemished many blemishes and HaShem executed judgments upon them. And see the comment on the verse (Exodus 32:8): ״סָרוּ מַהֵר מִן הַדֶּרֶךְ״, etc." "And it appears to me that not all of Israel agreed to the error; rather, a portion of them did, and a portion did not protest, and they were balanced regarding the matter, and a portion had no power to protest. And behold, from the precision of their saying <b>your Elohim</b> — and they did not say, perish the thought, ״our Elohim… who brought us out״ — this indicates that some of them said this to all of them, and some justified it and some did not justify it. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 42:6) that the mixed multitude who came up with Israel were the ones who did this, and necessarily one must say that Israel did not agree at the time of the deed, for if they had agreed then they too would have done it, since thought is efficacious in idolatry — and they also did not protest, and for this the totality of Israel was punished, aside from the few who had the ability, whose hearts HaShem tested and did not incline toward evil. And those publicly known for this matter are the tribe of Levi. And behold, after the calf was made, one must say that a portion of Israel went astray after it, as it is written (Exodus 32:28): ״וַיִּפֹּל מִן הָעָם כִּשְׁלֹשֶׁת אַלְפֵי אִישׁ״, and necessarily these were from Israel who fell, as is evident from His saying (Exodus 32:27): ״הִרְגוּ אִישׁ אֶת אָחִיו… וְאֶת קְרֹבוֹ״. If so, one must say that a portion of Israel worshipped idolatry with witnesses and warning."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:35:47.716860227Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}